{
  "_doc": [
    "Policy identity lock. One id@version names exactly one content hash, forever: change a policy's content and you must raise its version and lock the new pair. Entries are never rewritten - old ones are how a stored run's hash stays traceable after the policy moves on.",
    "policy_hash is the 64-hex SHA-256 over the None-free canonical IR. The top-level hash_scheme (sha256-canonical-v2) is the scheme of every entry that carries no hash_scheme of its own - all entries locked up to 2026-10-06; an entry locked since records its own hash_scheme. legacy_hashes are the 16-hex forms the same content carried under earlier code, so manifests written before 2026-10-06 still resolve: legacy16-exclude-none (runs up to 2026-08-31; it is also the first 16 hex of policy_hash), legacy16-valid-time (4950be1, 2026-09-01, a window of hours), legacy16-include-defaults (445bdbe 2026-09-01 to 2026-10-06).",
    "hashes (optional, per entry) records the SAME content's hash under a later canonical scheme, added by lock --update and never replacing policy_hash. Scheme sha256-canonical-v3 (2026-10-06) stores WGS84 points as lat/lon instead of projected metres; it differs from v2 only for geographic policies (today wgs84_taipei.yaml), so only that entry carries one.",
    "derived lists policies that exist only mid-flight (a hot-applied rule bumps generation and the hash), with the recipe to rebuild them.",
    "ir_schema pins what every IR field means when omitted; see guardrail.models.ir_schema_defaults and tests/test_policy_hash.py. A pin lists every field of its model: lock --update adds a new field whose omission means '<absent>' (the one change that moves no hash) and never alters an existing field's pin. The 2.1.0 fields (scope, layer, altitude_ref, issued_at, layers_merged, lat/lon) were added to the pre-existing models' pins on 2026-10-07 this way.",
    "Maintain with: python -m guardrail.bundle lock [--update]. Checked by tests/test_policy_hash.py."
  ],
